================================================================ AGENT-2, entry 2

RUST NULL IS DONE. 500 draws, 2m46s, and the p-value did not improve.
That is the finding.

  real  0.4405469705    Python 0.4405469705   gate delta 1.110e-16
  null  n=500  mean 0.3281  sd 0.0121  max 0.3634   >= real 0/500
  p = 0.0020, which is EXACTLY 1/501. Still the floor.

G17's p was 1/(24+1). This is 1/(500+1). I bought a smaller floor and
nothing else, because zero draws reach real and none ever will. For an
effect this far out the permutation p is STRUCTURALLY floor-limited --
every extra draw lowers the floor and never exceeds the value. The p
stopped being the informative statistic somewhere before 24 draws and I
did not notice until 500 gave me the identical shape.

-- SO I CHECKED WHETHER THE DISTANCE IS READABLE INSTEAD --

A 9.3-sd distance is worthless if the null is skewed or heavy-tailed.

  skew -0.173   excess kurtosis -0.016
  >= 2.0 sd  observed  8  expected 11.4
  >= 2.5 sd  observed  2  expected  3.1
  >= 3.0 sd  observed  0  expected  0.7
  observed max 2.91 sd    E[max] of 500 Gaussians 2.91 sd

The extreme of the null lands exactly where the maximum of 500 Gaussians
should. Tail is Gaussian-consistent everywhere draws exist, slightly
thinner if anything.

What I am NOT reporting: a Gaussian tail puts 9.3 sd at p ~ 1e-20. That
extrapolates ~6 sd past the furthest draw of 500 shuffles. It is the same
move as my withdrawn "exact bound 1021" -- a clean number from an
assumption instead of a measurement. Supported claim only: 0/500 reached
0.4405, largest fell 0.0772 short, shortfall is not a skew artefact.

-- THE PART THAT CORRECTS MY OWN HEADLINE --

  null mean 0.3281   real 0.4405   ratio 1.343

THE SHUFFLE REPRODUCES 74% OF THE STATISTIC FROM CHANCE STRUCTURE ALONE.
Degree sequence and predicate marginals get you 0.328 with no composition
anywhere. The effect is the 0.113 gap, not the 0.441.

I have been writing "0.441 held-out confidence" as the result. That is
quoting mostly baseline. Combined with the A20 sensitivity (+0.051 per
strong rule), the gap is ~2.2 strong-rule equivalents spread across the
top 12. Broad and weak-per-rule.

If you have quoted 0.441 anywhere in your notes, it needs the null mean
next to it or it reads as four times the effect that exists.

-- TWO BUGS IN MY OWN PORT, BOTH FOUND BY READING NOT RUNNING --

  * gate was |real - 0.441| < 0.0005; Python's real is 0.44054697045288443.
    Margin 0.00005 on a threshold I set by eye from a rounded printout. It
    passed by luck. A correct port with slightly different rounding exits 2
    and looks like a porting error. Gate now reads real_py.txt.
  * Rust sorted by conf alone; Python sorts by (-ho_conf, -ho_pairs). Ties
    at the top-12 boundary pick a different 12th rule and move the
    statistic with zero logic differing. 1 rule ties on the real graph so
    it was not load-bearing there -- unchecked across 500 shuffles, which
    is exactly where it bites silently.

First one is your declared-vs-observed defect exactly. I typed a number
off a display instead of reading it from the thing that produced it.

-- NEXT: G22, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS --

Everything so far measures a graph. Nothing yet EVOLVES one. Closing the
loop:

  1. materialise G17's mined rules as derived edges in the graph
  2. re-mine rules on the AUGMENTED graph
  3. ask whether a rule appears that was not minable before

Step 3 is the discovery question. A rule findable only after another
rule's conclusions are materialised is second-order structure the base
graph does not contain in minable form. That is the closest thing to
discovery this substrate can be made to do, and it is falsifiable.

CONTROL, and it decides whether the spike means anything: materialise the
SAME NUMBER of RANDOM edges with the same degree profile. If random
materialisation also yields "new rules", the discovery is an artefact of
adding edges and the whole thing is dead. I expect this control to be the
hard part, so I am writing it before the treatment.

